---
description: "Whole pixels in apps/web: no fractional px on a layout or border length"
globs: apps/web/**
alwaysApply: false
---

## Whole pixels

A layout or border length in `apps/web` is a whole number of CSS pixels.
Never `1.5px`, never `0.2px`, never `0.5px`.

### Why

A CSS pixel is not a device pixel. On a 2x Mac display, `1.5px` is exactly
three device pixels, so it looks deliberate to the person who wrote it. On a
1x display, which is most Windows hardware, there is no half pixel. The
browser rounds, and it does not round consistently: a `1.5px` border lands at
1px on one edge of the box and 2px on the opposite edge. A `0.2px` border
rounds away to nothing on some surfaces and to a hairline on others.

The result is an element that is visibly lopsided on the hardware most users
have, and correct only on the hardware the author has.

### What is banned

Any fractional `px` on a length that resolves to a painted edge or a box
dimension:

- `width`, `height`, `size`, and the `min-` / `max-` forms
- `padding`, `margin`, `gap`
- `top`, `right`, `bottom`, `left`, `inset`
- `border`, `border-*-width`, `outline`, `outline-width`, `outline-offset`

In every form the codebase uses: a CSS declaration (`padding: 1.5px`), a React
style object (`border: "0.2px solid transparent"`), and a Tailwind arbitrary
value (`p-[1.5px]`, `border-[0.5px]`, `ring-offset-[1.5px]`).

### What is allowed

Continuous values. The browser interpolates these rather than snapping them to
a device pixel, so a fraction is meaningful:

- blur and shadow radii — `blur(1.5px)`, `--chat-jump-dim-blur: 1.5px`
- translations inside a keyframe — `translateY(-0.25px)`, which the
  compositor tweens anyway
- unitless scale factors — `scale(0.96)`

`rem` and `em` lengths are also out of scope. They are the correct unit for
type and for anything that must scale with the root, and their computed px
value is expected to be fractional.

### The fix

Pick the whole pixel the author meant.

| Was | Use | Reason |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `border: "0.2px solid …"` | `border: "1px solid …"` | 0.2px is a fifth of a device pixel even at 2x. 1px is the hairline it was reaching for. |
| `padding: 1.5px` | `padding: 2px` | Keeps the ring heavier than a hairline and renders the same on 1x and 2x. |
| `p-[1.5px]` | `p-0.5` | 2px, and back on the 4pt scale. |

Prefer a Tailwind spacing step over an arbitrary value. `p-0.5` is 2px; the
fraction in the class name is a step on the 4pt scale, not a fraction of a
pixel.

### Guard

`apps/web/src/lib/utils/__tests__/whole-pixels.test.ts` scans every `.ts`,
`.tsx` and `.css` file under `apps/web/src` and fails on a fractional px in
any of the three forms above. Generated clients and test files are skipped.
